unists to a camp of hippies that's  just trying to spend more resources and  we have one national politician who  wrote use from the right wing in Denmark  hero you know the state shouldn't  interfere with people's happiness it's  up to the people themselves to create  their own happiness and we replied in  the same paper that if you're a national  politician that doesn't think you can  create framework conditions to improve  people's lives then why are you sitting  in Parliament you got a little mad over  that but overall the reactions has been  really good so what we try to do of  course we try to see what can we do in  order to increase the sense of community  relationships for people and and we we  did three things together with the city  first of all we completely eliminated  the fee to the local community center  there's a well-functioning community  center there is anything from bridge to  martial arts to patong but a lot of  people low-income groups were not able  to use the center because there were  fees so we eliminated that the second  initiative was to create more community  gardens or urban gardens it's very  popular in many countries unless you're  also here community gardens are good for  several reasons first of all they're  great to look at in the city it's also  an easy initiative to invite lonely p  into it doesn't carry the same social  stigma as other initiatives do it's  easier to my people in 20 come down down  this August we're going to have as  carrots doesn't hold this social stigma  of you being lonely and that's why we're  inviting you another thing is and that's  the timing issue the activities in the  community gardens take place at the  times where we know loneliness is the  worst spring summer right now what is  happening we're seeing you know  everybody's playing volleyball down by  the beach in Stanley Park everybody is  having out hanging out with their  friends those are the times of year  where lonely people feel lonely Nazz the  hardest because they can see the rest of  the community flourishing the last thing  was that we now have a group of  volunteers youth that are taking some of  our senior citizens around town on  bicycles such as this touring the the  town of sites that is important to our  senior citizens it could be a childhood  home where the senior citizen into  school but coming for all these things  is that they are trying to build tighter  stronger community having people meet  each other from different groups and all  of these initiatives are actually also  they're very simple the very low-tech  their cost efficient and these are just  from from our town we see range of  activities across the world that is  doing just the same and we're trying to  get inspiration for that as well and  that's also why i'm here today to get an  inspiration from you guys so i can bring  back home so we can still keep our first  place in the happiness rankings but just  in your international examples we see in  in the netherlands since 2006 they have  conducted one ish car called the  National neighbor day right so it's  something we actually used to do you  know having coffee with our neighbors  talking over the picket fence and so on  is something we've forgotten to do over  the last decades in Holland now since  2006 they put it on the agenda and you  might say it's funny that we should have  a national day to celebrate that or  become you know do that why don't we  just do it by ourselves but we also have  Christmas a tradition that brings people  together in families we might as well  have one that brings neighbors together  it's a big success in the New Orleans  thousands and thousands of cups of  coffee are being drunk neighbors alike  in Bristol they've conducted a whole  range of activities in the happy city  program one of them has been to hand out  happy city points when they see people  doing something nice to a stranger they  hand out happy city points which you can  purchase coffee in local stores for of  course there's some you know discussion  you know why don't people just do it by  themselves you really want them for  being nice they should just be nice and  that's true as well but I think it's an  interesting and creative initiative and  we can actually also find inspiration in  countries that most of us probably don't  think of of being very happy in  Afghanistan they have what is called the  mobile minute circus for kids and it  teaches them how to perform different  sorts of circus activities it teaches  them how to juggle it teaches them how  to do acrobatics but most importantly it  teaches them to engage in relationships  to be part of a community to enjoy other  people and it teaches them that being  part of something with other people can  create quality of life for themselves  and I think I'm about out of time now  but I look forward to the discussion  afterwards so thank you for listening  you
 

